[Music]  [Applause]  as well  yesterday we had nick wright on and uh  he's one of our favorites he's a co-host  of first things first  and uh he used to live in houston and  we somehow got around to barbecue  because he loves the kansas city chiefs  and he talked about houston barbecue is  not  very good come to kansas city for the  barbecue jj  stay for the championships that would be  my recommendation  well you have lived in houston so you  you are yeah i lived there for four  years and ate what they  listen houston folks i understand it's  been tough times  i empathize with you immensely but texas  barbecue  is fraudulent and not it's not like  bad compared to its reputation it's bad  compared to food  it's just not good  that's nick wright yesterday so paulie  goes i got somebody who's going to help  us here he's the barbecue edit  editor of texas monthly magazine daniel  vaughn joining us now  and the author of the prophets of smoked  meat a journey through texas barbecue  co-author of whole hog barbecue he's uh  uh sampled over 1800 barbecue joints  most of which are in texas and daniel  kind enough to join us daniel your  reaction to nick wright  calling texas barbecue fraudulent  oh man i heard it yesterday you just  played it for me now  like i feel personally attacked i mean  fraudulent like  is it fraudulent because like in in  texas and houston and austin and dallas  fort worth wherever you are  we cook with wood instead of cooking  with gas like most of the big names in  kansas city  is that what's fraudulent about texas  barbecue  i i just i can't can't even wrap my head  around it  okay kansas city gets credit for maybe  the best barbecue  first of all explain what kansas city  barbecue is and  does kansas city deserve that high of a  ranking  well what high of a ranking i mean i  don't put it anywhere like  as a high ranking okay um i mean i mean  to me obviously texas barbecue is number  one um  and so then from the area i'd say you go  to north carolina  south carolina the whole hog barbecue  over there like  that's better than anything you're going  to get in kansas city now kansas city  they got a lot of options right they got  much like texas barbecue they actually  have some similarities you get your  brisket  your pork get your pork ribs um some  sausage you get all those things right  in both texas and kansas city it's just  that in texas is the one place where you  can get a good version of sliced brisket  i mean in kansas city it's just it's so  uh it's so tough they gotta slice it  with a deli slicer before they put it on  your tray  i mean in kansas city they argue about  barbecue sauce they don't argue about  which place has the best meat they talk  about which place has the best sauce  people who are ordering from kansas city  barbecue joints or ordering sauce to be  delivered to their house not the  barbecue  well that's what i wondered about what  you put on the ribs  or do you put nothing on the ribs  well in texas uh i mean most barbecue  here is served with sauce on the side  everybody serves barbecue sauce but we  don't try to cover it up like kansas  city right we don't want to cover up the  meat we want the sauce to be on the side  so the meat can stand on its own because  generally the meat can stand on its own  uh which is not  the case in kansas city what about  memphis barbecue daniel  i mean memphis barbecue is good but much  like kansas city it's like  you know texas barbecue for example i'm  i just got a photo from kyrgyzstan sent  to me  this morning of a barbecue joint in in  kyrgyzstan doing texas-style barbecue  like last week they were standing uh  in riyadh saudi arabia they're standing  in line for texas barbecue  it's like kansas city and memphis they  can't even get beyond their own city  limits  uh seton you had a question for daniel  well yeah i just think it's interesting  that we keep  like keep saying like memphis barbecue  versus texas barbecue  or kansas city barbecue versus texas  barbecue  whereas you're taking like a population  of 30 million people  and then comparing it to a population of  500 000 people and being like yeah we're  better okay how about we do a city  all right so i mean  austin austin houston fort worth  all cities with better barbecue than any  other city  in america if i was coming  to texas for a week what city would i go  to for the best barbecue  man well i mean for the all-time best  barbecue i mean  we do a top 50 barbecue list every four  years at texas monthly  and snow's barbecues at the top and  they're right outside of austin  franklin barbecue is right there they're  right in austin so i'd have to say  austin is the best place as far as like  getting the biggest bang for your buck  what makes snows the best  uh just the fact that they do everything  well like you can usually tell somebody  uh when they're going to a barbecue  joint like which thing to order that's  really going to wow you  and it snows it's like you can't break  it down like that it's like everything  is going to wow  like everything is spectacular and then  of course when you go you get to  you know see one of the most famous  texas pit masters  uh tootsie tominets who's 85 years old  now will be 86 this year  doing her thing at snow's bbq so that's  part of what makes it special too  should you avoid the sides because you  need to focus on the meat  yeah yeah i mean sides bread like all  those things like pickles  pickles are medicinal like pickles are  going to help you eat more  but all that other stuff like i mean if  you're going  for like just a big time barbecue tour  in texas yeah you wanna you wanna stay  away from all the sides  is there good barbecue in the north  well i mean hometown barbecue in  brooklyn they do a fantastic  job so i've got to say yes and i will  say too like there is  good barbecue in kansas city there's a  guy tyler harp he runs  a heart barbecue there and actually he  was so mad about an article that i wrote  about kansas city barbecue that said  some unkind  things about it that he came to texas  with his dad he was mad he's like i'm  going to prove this guy wrong  and he goes to austin eats the barbecue  eats at several different places  goes back home to kansas city and  realizes that his life's mission now is  to bring  texas barbecue to people in kansas city  so if you do want good brisket in kansas  city there is the one place it's heart  barbecue  and it's because they're cooking texas  style barbecue uh what about  los angeles los angeles um  let's see uh horn barbecue well that's  not los angeles that's in oakland that's  that's certainly  uh high on my list of places to go visit  um i mean i haven't had anything that  like truly wowed me there but then  moose craft bbq in los angeles is a  place that i haven't been able to visit  yet so  there is some good barbecue out there  but of course it's it's going to be  probably texas style daniel thanks for  joining us i love you're fired up and  i'll make sure that nick  wright hears what you had to say all  right i appreciate that thank you that's  daniel vaughn the barbecue editor of  texas monthly magazine  wow he has sampled smoked meats  at over 1800 barbecue joints most of  which  are in texas how about the fact that he  wrote a book called the prophets of  smoked meat a journey through texas  barbecue  he co-authored authored a book the whole  hog  barbecue the gospel of carolina barbecue

